lso known as total lunar eclipses are  pretty rare like occurring up to once  every three years kind of rare  however blood moons and breath of the  wild are not that way I mean those  things can sometimes occur as often as  two nights in a row or more if we are to  assume that the planet Hyrule is on  let's call it planet H if we are to  assume that planet H operates at least  somewhat similarly to our own planet  Earth which I would assume it does given  it also has a similar Pope Sun moon day  and night cycle this would pose a  problem for the notion that breadth of  the wilds Blood Moon is purely  scientific in nature furthermore as NBC  stated a real-life Blood Moon occurs  when only red light is reaching the  moon's surface meaning the other 80% of  light isn't being reflected back at us  leaving to a much darker evening but in  breadth of the wild a blood moon doesn't  darken things up at all in fact the  world seems to brighten up with an eerie  red glow couple this with the clouds  suddenly and inexplicably changing their  speed and the repeated occurrences and  I'm convinced that breadth of the wilds  Blood Moon phenomenon isn't scientific  in nature at all it's magical oh yeah  and it also raises the dead which I'm  pretty sure is magical unless this is  actually the legend of zelda  Frankenstein's army or something like  but yeah the Blood Moon from breath of  the wild isn't the only time we've known  the moon to herald the rising of the  Dead rewind back in time to ocarina of  time I feel like I say that a lot in  this game when you're out and about  adventuring across hyrule field sooner  or later the sun will set and the moon  will rise and with it so do the dead as  soon as nightfall hits dozens of stall  children emerge from beneath the field  of Hyrule oh and by the way I did a  video with my buddy hyrule gamer last  Halloween regarding the strange  occurrence check it out if you want  links in the description below anyway  unless these undead beings just really  wanted to take a nice stroll under the  night sky and really didn't want a Sun  Tan the only thing that's changed from  the light of day is the light of the  Moon so okay why am i spending so much  time talking about the dead and the  light and the magical nature of the Moon  hold your horses I'm getting there  back in breath of the wild as many  people know Zelda has something specific  to say about the chilling night of the  Blood Moon she states that Ganon's power  rises to its peak under the Blood Moon  Ganon's power a magical Blood Moon  what's the correlation why should the  Moon react again and flexing on it a  little bit another clue we can ponder  from breath of the wild comes from the  final fight against calamity Ganon when  you're in combat beneath Hyrule Castle  with the Beast himself if it just so  happens to strike on the night of a  Blood Moon Ganon's health will be  refilled as if he had received no damage  at all as if he were rewound but  otherwise he has no way of healing  himself unless of course you're on hero  mode but that's besides the point  this begs the question if the Blood Moon  were truly under Ganon's control why  would he not use it to heal himself  every time you fight because if you  start the encounter and no Blood Moon  occurs he'll just receive damage and die  like normal implying he can't just  activate it whenever he wants even if  it's to save himself does this mean that  the Blood Moon happens outside of  Ganon's command what if it's not the  Moon reacting to Ganon's power but  rather Ganon's power reacting to the  moon but let's take a few steps back and  set something else up real quick we know  the moon is alive how from Majora's Mask  and yes I know that the world of termina  is most likely a parallel universe to  Hyrule which is to say it takes place  apart from the line  and universe that the usual games take  place in however due to its parallel  nature featuring the same yet different  characters and 